==English==
{{was wotd|2008|August|24}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|encur}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱers-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|incurren}}, from {{der|en|xno|encurir}}, {{der|en|frm|encourir}}, from {{der|en|fro|encorre}}, from {{der|en|la|incurrere}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈkɜː/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈkɝ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-incur.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-incur.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring upon]] oneself or [[expose]] oneself to, especially something [[inconvenient]], [[harmful]], or [[onerous]]; to become [[liable]] or [[subject]] to.
#: {{nearsyn|en|contract<qq:debts, etc.>}}
#: {{cot|en|experience|encounter|sustain<id:experience or suffer>}}
#: {{ux|en|Cruelty '''incurs''' calamity.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've '''incur'''red scorn and odium for my actions.}}
#* {{RQ:Montagu Miscellanea Spiritualia|treatise=11|section=II|sectionname=Some Rules whereby to Square Our Discourse, and an Expedient Offered towards the Correction of Medisance|page=135|passage=VVherefore I beſeech every one vvhom it may concerne, to put on a ſerious diſplicence, upon theſe occaſions, that they may not '''incurre''' this menace of ''[[w:Jesus|Chriſt]]'', ''VVoe be unto you that laugh novv'', but rather entitle themſelves to this promiſe of the ''Holy Ghoſt'', ''They ſhall laugh in the latter day''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|translator=Henry Graham Dakyns|title=The works of [[Xenophon]]|chapter=s:The Hellenica|section=[[s:Hellenica (Xenophon)/Book 5/Chapter 3 |Book 5, Chapter 3]]
|passage=[T]he master in his wrath may easily '''incur''' worse evil himself than he inflicts—[...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|author=w:Nicholas Machiavelli|translator=Ninian Hill Thomson|title=w:The Prince|section=[[s:The Prince (Hill Thomson)/Chapter XIX|Chapter XIX]]
|passage=And here it is to be noted that hatred is '''incurred''' as well on account of good actions as of bad;}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|legal|accounting}} To render (somebody, or oneself) [[liable]] or [[subject]] to.
##: {{syn|en|occasion}}
##: {{nearsyn|en|entail|invoke}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|author=w:Francis Colburn Adams|title=An Outcast|chapter=[[s:An Outcast/Chapter VII|Chapter VII]]
|passage=The least neglect of duty will '''incur'''[...] the penalty of thirty-nine well laid on in the morning.}}
##* {{RQ:Chapman Eastward Hoe|passage=Lest you '''incur''' me much more damage in my fame than you have done me pleasure in preserving my life.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[enter]] or pass into.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[fall]] within a [[period]] or [[scope]]; to [[occur]]; to [[run into]] [[danger]].

